## 5. Real Cost Breakdown (at 70–100 posts/day)

| Cost item | Amount | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| YouTube Data API | **$0** | Free; quota-based, 6 free uploads/day before a free quota increase request is needed |
| Instagram + Facebook Graph API | **$0** | Free to call; Instagram caps at 100 API-published posts/24hr per account — worth monitoring as volume grows |
| X (Twitter) API | **~$90–180/month** | Pay-per-post: $0.015 plain text, $0.20 if the post contains a link (TV9 content will mostly include links back to articles/videos) |
| AWS hosting (once deployed) | **~$65–95/month** | ECS Fargate, managed Postgres (RDS), S3, Secrets Manager — all fully managed to minimize ongoing ops work |
| **Third-party alternative, for comparison** | **$0–99/month extra** | What a hosted tool like Postiz Cloud or Ayrshare would have cost *in addition to* the X fees above |

**The X API fee is the dominant, unavoidable cost regardless of which path was chosen.** The build-vs-buy decision affected engineering time and long-term ownership — not this line item.

