## 10. Anticipated Questions

**"What happens if this breaks in production?"**
Every platform call is wrapped in error handling that records exactly what failed and why. Nothing is a black box — any TV9 engineer can read the failure reason directly from the dashboard or database, rather than guessing.

**"Is this secure?"**
OAuth tokens are never stored in the main database — only a reference to where the real credential lives. Locally that's a local file for development; in production it moves to AWS Secrets Manager, meaning credentials are never exposed in code, logs, or the database itself.

**"What's the realistic timeline to have all 4 platforms live in production?"**
Roughly 3–4 more weeks of development (Instagram, Facebook, X adapters), run in parallel with Meta's review process, followed by AWS deployment and end-to-end testing.

**"Why not just use ChatGPT/AI to build the platforms' apps for us instantly?"**
Every platform (YouTube, Meta, X) requires manual developer registration, security review, and credential setup that only TV9 (as the account owner) can complete — no engineering approach skips this. What's been accelerated is the code itself, not the platform-side approval process.